-- Q4448: Professional Skepticism and Judgment [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 200, professional skepticism requires critically assessing audit evidence rather than accepting explanations without corroboration. An unusual spike in year-end revenue warrants further investigation, including examining shipping documents, sales contracts, and confirming with customers to determine whether the transactions are legitimate.
Wrong (A): Accepting management''s explanation without corroboration is the opposite of professional skepticism. An unusual revenue pattern demands independent verification.
Wrong (C): Reducing substantive testing after identifying a variance is counterintuitive. The variance signals increased risk requiring more testing, not less.
Wrong (D): Issuing a qualified opinion based solely on an unusual variance without investigation is premature. The auditor must first gather sufficient evidence to determine whether a misstatement exists.$EXPL$
