-- Q4450: Professional Skepticism and Judgment [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Confirmation bias is the tendency to search for, interpret, and recall information that confirms pre-existing beliefs. Under AU-C 200, this bias directly threatens professional skepticism by causing auditors to overweight evidence supporting management''s assertions and dismiss contradictory evidence.
Wrong (A): Anchoring bias causes over-reliance on an initial piece of information when making subsequent judgments, not favoritism toward confirming evidence.
Wrong (C): Availability bias leads auditors to overweight information that is easily recalled or readily available, rather than systematically favoring confirming evidence.
Wrong (D): Overconfidence bias involves excessive certainty in one''s own judgments or abilities, which is distinct from selectively favoring confirming information.$EXPL$
